<%# The account layer (Belegungstabelle F47): the pages of "Mein Konto", rising from the
    reader's name at the foot of the sidebar up to the logo. The page behind stays; only
    a click on an entry leaves it. Until 2026-11-12 the name opened a small menu of four
    entries, and the full list showed only after one of them had been clicked.

    It looks like the sidebar of an account page, because that is what an entry leads
    to: the same list (common/_account_nav_list), the same surface. An organisation's
    recognition colour does not reach it: the account stands above the organisations.

    Where it stands: in the sidebar's stage (layouts/application), the one grid cell it
    shares with the app's navigation and the utility row, which it covers. The logo above
    and the name below stay; the name is its handle. sidebar_layer_controller opens and
    closes it (the name, the cross, Escape, a click outside), makes what it covers inert
    and gives the focus to its first entry; components/sidebar_layer.css is the slide.

    Rendered hidden on every page of an organisation: six links and no query, so a click
    answers at once. Without JavaScript it stays hidden and the name is a plain link to
    the account page, whose sidebar shows the same list. %>
<nav id="account-layer" class="sidebar-layer" hidden
     aria-label="<%= t('navigation.account_section') %>"
     data-sidebar-layer-target="panel">
  <div class="min-h-0 flex-1 overflow-y-auto px-4 pt-6 scrollbars">
    <%= render 'common/account_nav_list', layer: true %>
  </div>

  <%# Signing out stays where it was: the last thing above the name. %>
  <div class="flex-shrink-0 border-t border-cream-300 px-4 py-2">
    <%= link_to destroy_user_session_path, class: sidebar_row_classes(active: false), data: { turbo_method: :delete } do %>
      <i class="bi bi-box-arrow-right <%= sidebar_row_icon_classes %>" aria-hidden="true"></i>
      <span><%= t('navigation.logout') %></span>
    <% end %>
  </div>
</nav>
